<section id="A3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.4 </span>Discussion on RL Tricks</h3>

<div id="A3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We also review some widely adopted RL tricks, such as: increasing the number of
rollout samples, raising the training temperature, more than three actions case.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A3.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bullet" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bullet</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="A3.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The number of rollout samples</span>: a larger <math id="A3.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> leads to more stable optimization and
does not affect our main findings and conclusions, the two-stage dynamic of exploitation and exploration.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A3.F5" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4">
<figure id="A3.F5.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.04028v1/t_G_2_r1_0.9_r2_1.0_r1_0_1.8_0.5_2.5.svg" id="A3.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:141/136;" width="141" height="136"></object>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4">
<figure id="A3.F5.fig2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.04028v1/t_G_3_r1_0.9_r2_1.0_r1_0_1.8_0.5_2.5.svg" id="A3.F5.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:141/136;" width="141" height="136"></object>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4">
<figure id="A3.F5.fig3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.04028v1/t_G_5_r1_0.9_r2_1.0_r1_0_1.8_0.5_2.5.svg" id="A3.F5.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:141/136;" width="141" height="136"></object>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4">
<figure id="A3.F5.fig4" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.04028v1/t_G_10_r1_0.9_r2_1.0_r1_0_1.8_0.5_2.5.svg" id="A3.F5.g4" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:141/136;" width="141" height="136"></object>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>Dynamics of the policy probability mass during optimization for different numbers of rollout samples (<math id="A3.F5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[2,3,5,10]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[2,3,5,10]</annotation></semantics></math>), with action rewards <math id="A3.F5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> and initial policy probabilities <math id="A3.F5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> held constant.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A3.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A3.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bullet" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bullet</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="A3.SS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Raising the training temperature</span>: according to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">An et al. [2025]</a></cite>, increasing the sampling temperature enhances the diversity of generated outcomes. Consequently, employing a higher temperature is advisable to obtain a more varied set of trajectories for model training. The default temperature value in our other experiments is <math id="A3.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=1.0</annotation></semantics></math>, that is <math id="A3.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi(\cdot)=\text{Softmax}(\mathbf{z}/\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>Softmax</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi(\cdot)=\text{Softmax}(\mathbf{z}/\tau)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A3.F6" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4">
<figure id="A3.F6.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.04028v1/t_G_2_r1_0.9_r2_1.0_r1_0_1.8_0.5_2.5.svg" id="A3.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:141/136;" width="141" height="136"></object>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4">
<figure id="A3.F6.fig2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.04028v1/t_tau_2.svg" id="A3.F6.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:141/136;" width="141" height="136"></object>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4">
<figure id="A3.F6.fig3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.04028v1/t_tau_5.svg" id="A3.F6.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:141/136;" width="141" height="136"></object>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4">
<figure id="A3.F6.fig4" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.04028v1/t_tau_20.svg" id="A3.F6.g4" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:141/136;" width="141" height="136"></object>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span>Dynamics of the policy probability mass during optimization for different training temperature values (<math id="A3.F6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[1,2,5,20]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[1,2,5,20]</annotation></semantics></math>), with action rewards <math id="A3.F6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> and initial policy probabilities <math id="A3.F6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> held constant.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A3.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A3.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bullet" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bullet</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="A3.SS4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">More than three actions case</span>: from Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1 (The Expected Logits Update). ‣ 3.1 A Two-Stage Dynamic: Exploitation and Exploration ‣ 3 Probability Mass Dynamics ‣ The Debate on RLVR Reasoning Capability Boundary: Shrinkage, Expansion, or Both? A Two-Stage Dynamic View" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we have: <math id="A3.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}(\Delta z(a_{i}))=\eta\pi(a_{i})\left[(1-\pi(a_{i}))\hat{A}(a_{i})-\sum_{j\neq i}^{4}\pi(a_{j})\hat{A}(a_{j})\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mn>4</mn></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}(\Delta z(a_{i}))=\eta\pi(a_{i})\left[(1-\pi(a_{i}))\hat{A}(a_{i})-\sum_{j\neq i}^{4}\pi(a_{j})\hat{A}(a_{j})\right]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Denote the action with the largest <math id="A3.SS4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="A3.SS4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{\text{max}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mtext>max</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{\text{max}}</annotation></semantics></math> and the action with the smallest <math id="A3.SS4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="A3.SS4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{\text{min}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mtext>min</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{\text{min}}</annotation></semantics></math>. It can be readily shown that <math id="A3.SS4.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}(\Delta z(a_{\text{max}}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mtext>max</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}(\Delta z(a_{\text{max}}))</annotation></semantics></math> is always greater than or equal to 0, while <math id="A3.SS4.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}(\Delta z(a_{\text{min}}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mtext>min</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}(\Delta z(a_{\text{min}}))</annotation></semantics></math> is consistently less than 0. For other actions, the probabilities generally exhibit a two-stage dynamic.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A3.F7" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4">
<figure id="A3.F7.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.04028v1/a4.svg" id="A3.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:141/136;" width="141" height="136"></object>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4">
<figure id="A3.F7.fig2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.04028v1/a3.svg" id="A3.F7.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:141/136;" width="141" height="136"></object>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4">
<figure id="A3.F7.fig3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.04028v1/a2.svg" id="A3.F7.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:141/136;" width="141" height="136"></object>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4">
<figure id="A3.F7.fig4" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.04028v1/a1.svg" id="A3.F7.g4" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:141/136;" width="141" height="136"></object>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 7: </span>Dynamics of the policy probability mass during optimization for action space consists of four actions, with action rewards <math id="A3.F7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> and initial policy probabilities <math id="A3.F7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> held constant.</figcaption>
